# wfg-log 1
# fixture midi — three MIDI cues fired, and nothing reaches a port.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. A MIDI cue's whole visible effect is bytes leaving a
# cable, and a machine replaying a log has no cable: JUCE cannot even make a
# virtual port on Windows, and no CI runner has a MIDI interface at all. So the
# same rule the mounts and the triggers already follow applies here, and this is
# what says it holds — the SENDER is a seam the cue layer knows nothing about,
# and a replay installs none. The runs reproduce, the log reproduces, and no
# byte goes anywhere.
#
# THE PORT IS THE SHOW'S NAME AND NOT A DEVICE (PRD 4.10). <MidiPorts> declares
# "Lights" and "The desk"; which cable each is on is --midi-out's answer and a
# fact about the building. So a show travels, and this file replays on a machine
# that has never heard of either.
#
# GO on the first cue: a program change on channel three. Standby moves on.
A 0 0 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"MD000001"
#
# A memo-shaped ending: a MIDI cue whose wait is `none` is done on the tick
# after it fires, which is when a report is allowed to leave.
A 1 1 engine run.ended s:"MD000001"
#
# GO on the second: a system-exclusive dump, and a wait of `sent`. The wait is
# the difference between the two kinds of MIDI cue there are — this one is not
# done until the bytes have gone, which on a real rig is a queue and a thread
# and about thirty milliseconds for a hundred bytes.
#
# WITH NO SENDER THERE IS NOTHING TO WAIT FOR, so it too finishes on the next
# tick — and that is the honest answer rather than a hang: the cue asked for
# confirmation that the message left this machine, and on a machine with no port
# nothing was ever queued to leave.
A 100 2 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"MD000002"
A 101 3 engine run.ended s:"MD000002"
#
# GO on the third, which names a port nothing was bound to. On a real rig this
# fails with `no-port` while the rest of the show runs — a rig that has not been
# patched yet is a rehearsal, not a broken show.
#
# HERE IT DOES NOT FAIL, and the difference is worth having written down: with
# no sender at all there is nobody to say the port is missing, so the cue is
# applied and finishes like the two above. A replay reproduces what the ENGINE
# decided; whether a cable existed is not one of the engine's decisions.
A 200 4 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"MD000003"
A 201 5 engine run.ended s:"MD000003"
#
# And the memo that follows, so the standby has somewhere to have moved to.
A 300 6 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"MD000004"
A 301 7 engine run.ended s:"MD000004"
